# 2026-08-31 — NO BUMP. src/jsonld/ changed and no row moves, deliberately.
#   `toJsonLd` now writes the class of an inline nested node — cascade:EmergencyContact,
#   cascade:Address, cascade:PharmacyInfo, cascade:RecordSummary — where before it wrote
#   the node's children and no rdf:type. None of that is new vocabulary: all four classes
#   have been in core since v2.2 and this SDK has written them in TURTLE all along. What
#   changed is that the JSON-LD writer now reads the class from the term instead of not
#   reading it at all, so bumping core would claim support that already existed in one
#   format and was missing in the other.
#   The term is now the source for BOTH formats: defineTerm builds jsonLdFor and
#   fromJsonLdValue beside outputsFor, and src/jsonld/converter.ts forks on termFor(key)
#   above the PROPERTY_PREDICATES lookup. A field that is termed no longer needs a row in
#   that table to survive being written, which is what lets the table shrink (#46).

# 2026-08-31 — NO BUMP. src/models/ changed and no row moves, deliberately.
#   The new directory is src/models/validators/, which declares no vocabulary: it
#   transcribes constraints the shapes ALREADY state — sh:minCount, sh:maxCount,
#   sh:minLength — into a form validate() can read at runtime. Nothing here adds
#   a class, a predicate or a property, and the models themselves are untouched
#   and still compile to `export {};`.
#   What it changes is WHERE a rule lives. A term owns facts about a PREDICATE,
#   true wherever it appears; a per-record-type validator owns facts about a
#   RECORD TYPE — an sh:minCount is scoped to one node shape — and rules spanning
#   two fields, which no per-field constraint can state. Bumping a vocabulary row
#   for that would claim support for something spec did not publish.

# 2026-08-31 — NO BUMP. sh:minLength is now enforced, and no row moves.
#   The constraint was already published: 30 sh:property blocks across the four
#   vendored shape files declare one, over 28 distinct predicates, and every one
#   is sh:minLength 1 — including the two predicates that appear in two shapes,
#   dct:title and health:conditionName, which agree. This SDK is catching up to
#   what spec already says, not asserting anything new.
#   It landed on the TERM rather than in a validator's constraint table, because
#   that measurement says it is a fact about a PREDICATE and not about any one
#   record type. clinical:ProcedureShape is the case that separates the two:
#   clinical:procedureName carries sh:minLength 1 and NO sh:minCount, so an
#   absent procedure name conforms and a present empty one does not.
#   CHARACTERS, NOT CONTENT. SHACL measures the value node converted to string,
#   so "  " is length 2 and conforms. The check does not trim. A whitespace-only
#   name is a real defect and sh:minLength is not the constraint that states it;
#   sh:pattern is, and no shape declares one. Trimming here would reject records
#   pyshacl accepts, which is exactly the divergence the fixture contract exists
#   to catch.

